1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
The most powerful assault rifle ever used in combat.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
For more than 30 years, it has been a symbol of American strength worldwide.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:14,000
It has outlived controversy, political infighting, and multiple redesign efforts.

4
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Yet its power and sharp aim still gives it the distinction of the military's gun of choice.

5
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Now, the M16 on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,980
The gun has played a critical role in history.

7
00:00:38,580 --> 00:00:40,900
An invention which has been praised and denounced,

8
00:00:41,260 --> 00:00:42,860
served hero and villain alike,

9
00:00:43,220 --> 00:00:45,500
and carries with it moral responsibility.

10
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:49,580
To understand the gun is to better understand history.

11
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:57,060
At first glance, it looks like a toy, not a real gun.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,580
Its aluminum and fiberglass body weighs a shade more than eight pounds, measures barely

13
00:01:06,581 --> 00:01:14,240
more than a yard, and when fired, has a recoil so gentle a 12-year-old could handle it.

14
00:01:14,241 --> 00:01:17,480
Appearances here are most definitely deceiving.

15
00:01:17,481 --> 00:01:20,720
This gun's destructive power is undeniable.

16
00:01:20,721 --> 00:01:26,560
Though the M16 became famous as Vietnam's gun, weapon designers had played with the

17
00:01:26,561 --> 00:01:32,590
concept of a lightweight, high-powered automatic fire weapon since at least the 1940s.

18
00:01:32,591 --> 00:01:33,291
But

19
00:01:33,292 --> 00:01:37,320
for the better part of a decade, it remained little more than a good idea.

20
00:01:37,321 --> 00:01:45,000
Then, in the 1950s, severe setbacks in the early days of the Korean War forced the United

21
00:01:45,001 --> 00:01:48,740
States to seriously rethink the nature of the combat rifle.

22
00:01:48,741 --> 00:01:55,240
In Korea, there was just these human wave attacks by the Chinese.

23
00:01:56,100 --> 00:02:01,520
The Chinese suffered one million casualties in Korea,

24
00:02:02,580 --> 00:02:05,800
mainly inflicted by artillery, but certainly a lot by small arms.

25
00:02:06,620 --> 00:02:12,160
But when you have hundreds and hundreds and thousands and thousands of Chinese coming up a hill at you,

26
00:02:12,460 --> 00:02:13,360
you want firepower.

27
00:02:13,361 --> 00:02:19,920
American infantry soldiers in Korea didn't have that firepower.

28
00:02:20,240 --> 00:02:26,380
They were armed with World War II weapons, the Grand M1 Rifle and the M1 Carbine.

29
00:02:26,920 --> 00:02:34,400
Though the Chinese were also armed with older weapons, the M1 and the M1 Carbine were no match for the Chinese onslaught.

30
00:02:34,401 --> 00:02:46,740
The highly respected M1 rifle was semi-automatic, fed by a clip that held 8 .30 caliber cartridges, and had a kill range of 1,000 yards.

31
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:54,040
But a soldier with an M1 could fire a maximum of about 50 aimed shots per minute, not nearly enough for Korea.

32
00:02:54,720 --> 00:02:58,640
The M1 carbine with its pistol cartridge was even less appropriate.

33
00:02:58,641 --> 00:03:08,051
There are instances in Korea, particularly in the wintertime, where people get a lot of clothes on, of Marines and soldiers shooting Chinese soldiers, them getting

34
00:03:08,052 --> 00:03:13,940
back up and dusting themselves off and coming on because the round would not penetrate their clothing.

35
00:03:13,941 --> 00:03:21,320
The kind of close combat fighting soldiers experienced in Korea dramatized the need for a gun like the M16.

36
00:03:22,180 --> 00:03:23,420
A much lighter weapon.

37
00:03:24,060 --> 00:03:31,500
A weapon that could compensate for a gunner's inaccuracy by firing a spray of bullets at a shorter range, say 300 yards.

38
00:03:32,360 --> 00:03:37,480
But that idea violated one of the American military's favorite and most enduring myths.

39
00:03:43,940 --> 00:03:50,940
According to popular legend, it was expert American sharpshooters who helped defeat the

40
00:03:50,941 --> 00:03:53,463
British during the Revolutionary War.

41
00:03:53,464 --> 00:03:56,940
The Minutemen took out their targets at long range.

42
00:03:56,941 --> 00:04:00,940
One shot, one kill.

43
00:04:04,940 --> 00:04:10,940
That proud myth was bolstered during World War I by the legend of Sergeant Alvin York.

44
00:04:10,941 --> 00:04:14,520
He apparently tricked the enemy by imitating the call of a turkey.

45
00:04:15,420 --> 00:04:21,620
When the German soldiers poked their heads out of the trenches, Sergeant York picked them off, one by one.

46
00:04:26,420 --> 00:04:30,480
The idea of a lone marksman with a heavy caliber long-range weapon,

47
00:04:30,481 --> 00:04:35,100
almost single-handedly defeating the enemy, lasted until 1950.

48
00:04:35,101 --> 00:04:45,240
It was then the Army did a groundbreaking study on exactly how the infantry fights.

49
00:04:45,780 --> 00:04:56,460
It was probably the first time that a systematic study was done on how infantry riflemen engage targets in the field.

50
00:04:56,680 --> 00:05:03,440
We didn't really have the kind of information to indicate that until the Korean conflict, probably.

51
00:05:05,100 --> 00:05:12,100
During the 1950s, William Davis was chief of the Small Arms Branch at the military's Aberdeen Proving Grounds,

52
00:05:12,101 --> 00:05:17,100
the facility for testing infantry weapons.

53
00:05:17,101 --> 00:05:23,100
According to new studies, most soldiers held their fire until the enemy was within 200 yards.

54
00:05:23,101 --> 00:05:28,100
The average soldier waited until the enemy was 120 yards away.

55
00:05:28,101 --> 00:05:34,100
At that distance, the kill ratio dropped significantly, even for expert marksmen.

56
00:05:35,100 --> 00:05:40,340
You can argue either that the opportunities did not present themselves very often

57
00:05:40,341 --> 00:05:47,540
or that troops tended not to fire at targets that they considered beyond their effective range.

58
00:05:49,420 --> 00:05:51,900
Whatever the reason, the facts were clear.

59
00:05:52,260 --> 00:05:54,160
A long-range rifle was unnecessary.

60
00:05:55,160 --> 00:05:58,500
Despite the new study, few of the old hands at Army Ordnance

61
00:05:58,501 --> 00:06:00,780
wanted to give up the myth of the lone marksman.

62
00:06:01,260 --> 00:06:02,840
So, they compromised.

63
00:06:02,841 --> 00:06:09,020
New weapons for the American infantrymen are tested at Aberdeen Proving Grounds.

64
00:06:09,240 --> 00:06:14,280
The new standard rifle features interchangeable barrels that enable it to replace the old carving,

65
00:06:14,780 --> 00:06:19,180
the gun, the M1, the submachine gun, and the Browning automatic rifle.

66
00:06:20,340 --> 00:06:22,420
They called it the M14.

67
00:06:23,140 --> 00:06:26,820
It was essentially an M1 with a 20-round magazine hooked underneath.

68
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:31,460
The M14 boosted firepower, but it had a definite downside.

69
00:06:31,461 --> 00:06:37,220
In the full automatic mode, a soldier's aim was true only for the first shot.

70
00:06:38,980 --> 00:06:41,000
The problem with the M14 is the recoil.

71
00:06:41,800 --> 00:06:46,160
And if I go fully automatic with the M14, the first round is going to be dead on.

72
00:06:46,280 --> 00:06:47,880
The second round is going to be over your head.

73
00:06:47,980 --> 00:06:50,000
The third round is going to be way over your head.

74
00:06:50,360 --> 00:06:53,280
Anything after that, the gun's going to be an anti-aircraft gun.

75
00:06:53,281 --> 00:07:04,080
Nevertheless, in semi-automatic mode, the M14 was a distinct improvement over the M1, and inventories were low.

76
00:07:04,780 --> 00:07:07,420
The Army needed more guns, and fast.

77
00:07:08,360 --> 00:07:11,520
The M14 was the Army's only practical option.

78
00:07:12,160 --> 00:07:17,600
It was about to become the military's next official weapon when it got some unexpected competition.

79
00:07:17,601 --> 00:07:21,420
a futuristic-looking gun called the AR-15,

80
00:07:22,240 --> 00:07:25,100
the weapon destined to become the M16.

81
00:07:26,760 --> 00:07:30,960
To win that contest, the AR-15 would have to go to war,

82
00:07:31,460 --> 00:07:35,120
not against a foreign enemy, but against Army bureaucracy.

83
00:07:39,200 --> 00:07:43,340
In 1884, Hiram Maxim invented the Maxim gun,

84
00:07:43,341 --> 00:07:47,200
the first automatic weapon later to be called the machine gun.

85
00:07:47,600 --> 00:07:50,300
The M16 will continue on Modern Marvels.

86
00:07:52,680 --> 00:07:56,460
Just about everyone in the world knows the name Samuel Colt.

87
00:07:57,040 --> 00:08:01,000
He created the Peacemaker, the six-shooter that tamed the Wild West.

88
00:08:02,160 --> 00:08:04,080
Many know the name John Browning.

89
00:08:04,600 --> 00:08:08,200
He invented three of America's most successful military weapons,

90
00:08:08,700 --> 00:08:11,080
including the Browning Automatic Rifle.

91
00:08:12,080 --> 00:08:16,860
Few people, however, even gun fanciers, know the name Eugene Stoner,

92
00:08:16,861 --> 00:08:19,120
the father of the M16.

93
00:08:21,020 --> 00:08:24,640
Gene Stoner was a genius, an engineering genius of the first order.

94
00:08:25,560 --> 00:08:31,860
Stoner was probably the most gifted small arms designer since Browning.

95
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:37,780
In the early 1950s, Stoner was the chief engineer and designer

96
00:08:37,781 --> 00:08:42,620
at a startup gun company called Armalite, a division of Fairchild Aircraft.

97
00:08:42,621 --> 00:08:50,620
One of his first innovations was using new lightweight materials from the aircraft industry in his designs.

98
00:08:50,621 --> 00:08:55,620
Plastics that were impervious to the elements and came in a rainbow of colors.

99
00:08:55,621 --> 00:09:01,620
Fiberglass and stronger anodized aluminum alloys.

100
00:09:01,621 --> 00:09:05,620
Eugene Stoner died in 1997.

101
00:09:05,621 --> 00:09:10,620
In 1988, he was interviewed by the Smithsonian Institute.

102
00:09:10,621 --> 00:09:19,940
I think my aircraft background experience allowed me to get into some of these lightweight materials.

103
00:09:20,100 --> 00:09:25,760
For instance, forged aluminum receivers, which were rather unknown at the time in weapons.

104
00:09:26,320 --> 00:09:32,740
But it was nothing particularly new to what I've been doing all along in the aircraft equipment and the fiberglass and all that.

105
00:09:34,800 --> 00:09:39,620
Eugene Stoner and the Armolite Company got the Army's attention with some of his earliest designs.

106
00:09:40,620 --> 00:09:44,780
particularly for a lightweight rifle designated the AR-10.

107
00:09:45,640 --> 00:09:53,820
It weighed only 7 pounds and was capable of shooting 20 rounds of powerful, long-range 7.62mm bullets.

108
00:09:54,780 --> 00:09:58,660
In one test, however, a bullet split the barrel of a prototype,

109
00:09:59,420 --> 00:10:02,800
exploding out the side and missing the shooter's hand by inches.

110
00:10:03,640 --> 00:10:05,720
Though the AR-10 showed great promise,

111
00:10:05,840 --> 00:10:09,760
conservative Army ordnance officials chose not to develop it further.

112
00:10:10,620 --> 00:10:15,760
They were unimpressed with it. It was just too new.

113
00:10:16,440 --> 00:10:21,860
And again, this thing about the M-14 worked. So why change?

114
00:10:21,861 --> 00:10:28,780
My opinion is that they had to get the M-14 in production because they had to feed Springfield Armory and so forth.

115
00:10:28,920 --> 00:10:30,600
There's always that consideration.

116
00:10:30,980 --> 00:10:36,480
So they touted the M-14 as being the answer to a family of weapons.

117
00:10:36,481 --> 00:10:45,980
It was supposed to take the place of the M1 rifle, the carbine, the grease gun, you know, the .45 caliber submachine gun, and the BAR.

118
00:10:47,320 --> 00:10:55,000
Everybody that was in the business knew it was only going to take the place of the M1, because it had to fire it full automatic.

119
00:10:55,340 --> 00:10:57,080
There was hardly anyone that could handle it.

120
00:10:57,081 --> 00:11:02,060
they always made very favorable demonstrations with it

121
00:11:02,061 --> 00:11:05,980
because they had people that were very, very highly trained

122
00:11:05,981 --> 00:11:07,440
firing the thing full-automatic firing.

123
00:11:07,540 --> 00:11:08,660
They could put on a pretty good show,

124
00:11:08,780 --> 00:11:11,880
but when they tried to give it to ordinary troops, it was just useless.

125
00:11:13,940 --> 00:11:16,980
Though Army ordnance had turned down Stoner's designs,

126
00:11:16,981 --> 00:11:19,920
his guns had impressed someone with influence,

127
00:11:20,380 --> 00:11:22,620
a four-star general, William Wyman.

128
00:11:23,340 --> 00:11:28,500
In 1957, he paid a surprise visit to Armor Light's plant in Hollywood, California.

129
00:11:29,680 --> 00:11:33,860
And he just came in almost unannounced with a whole entourage and said,

130
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:38,940
hey, how would you like to get in on a rifle program for me?

131
00:11:39,040 --> 00:11:40,940
And I said, well, tell me about it.

132
00:11:41,760 --> 00:11:47,140
With Wyman's blessing, Eugene Stoner transformed his radical concepts into a brand new weapon.

133
00:11:47,680 --> 00:11:49,460
It was called the AR-15.

134
00:11:50,220 --> 00:11:52,140
It was a gun with a special destiny.

135
00:11:52,620 --> 00:11:55,620
a gun which would ultimately become the M16.

136
00:11:56,620 --> 00:12:01,100
This sexy space-age marvel was primarily made of fiberglass and aluminum.

137
00:12:02,020 --> 00:12:04,360
Only the barrel and a few other parts were steel.

138
00:12:05,140 --> 00:12:10,300
It could fire a magazine of 25 bullets, either semi-automatic or fully automatic.

139
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:18,780
The AR-15 was powered by an ingenious gas system that siphoned gas from the exploding charge out of the barrel,

140
00:12:18,781 --> 00:12:24,020
then sent its power back down a gas tube to force the bolt carrier to the rear.

141
00:12:25,260 --> 00:12:30,420
That ejected spent cartridges, making room for fresh ammo to pop up from the magazine.

142
00:12:32,300 --> 00:12:34,140
This is the original ER-15.

143
00:12:34,740 --> 00:12:36,420
As you can see, it's rather small.

144
00:12:36,540 --> 00:12:40,180
It weighs less, well, it weighs around 5 pounds, right about 5 pounds.

145
00:12:40,740 --> 00:12:44,500
The distinguishing characteristics are that this portion here is called Bakelite.

146
00:12:45,020 --> 00:12:46,400
It's an artificial material.

147
00:12:46,940 --> 00:12:48,720
Very fragile, however.

148
00:12:49,020 --> 00:12:50,520
You can break this very easily.

149
00:12:51,300 --> 00:12:56,460
And then it has a three-pronged flash suppressor up here in front,

150
00:12:56,880 --> 00:13:00,180
but as you can see, the barrel is very, very, very thin.

151
00:13:01,720 --> 00:13:03,640
It's also a fully automatic weapon,

152
00:13:04,320 --> 00:13:09,300
but this is flimsy, and it needed to be soldier-proofed.

153
00:13:11,100 --> 00:13:13,960
The ammunition itself was another innovation.

154
00:13:14,800 --> 00:13:17,560
The AR-15 used .22 caliber bullets.

155
00:13:18,480 --> 00:13:25,180
Small caliber bullets couldn't fly as far or hit long-range targets as hard as the large caliber bullets the Army preferred.

156
00:13:26,220 --> 00:13:29,020
Nevertheless, they offered an important advantage.

157
00:13:30,500 --> 00:13:37,900
As I recall, the M-14 with 100 rounds of ammunition was sort of a standard combat load,

158
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,940
and it weighed, I think, about 17 pounds, something on that order.

159
00:13:40,941 --> 00:13:45,900
whereas an AR-15 could carry 280 rounds of ammunition

160
00:13:45,901 --> 00:13:49,600
or nearly three times as much ammunition for the same combat load,

161
00:13:49,680 --> 00:13:54,040
and that was probably the most significant thing about the AR-15 system.

162
00:13:56,060 --> 00:14:01,720
Despite the AR-15's potential, Army ordnance, headed by Dr. Frederick H. Carton,

163
00:14:02,060 --> 00:14:03,620
did not order Stoner's gun.

164
00:14:04,420 --> 00:14:08,040
It was believed Carton didn't like outsiders developing Army guns,

165
00:14:08,041 --> 00:14:10,720
and he didn't like small-caliber weapons.

166
00:14:10,940 --> 00:14:19,400
There is a strong feeling among some people at that time that a reduction in caliber and

167
00:14:19,401 --> 00:14:24,700
power of the infantry rifle was a step backward.

168
00:14:24,701 --> 00:14:30,020
Some AR-15 advocates believed Carton was looking for any excuse to eliminate the new gun from

169
00:14:30,021 --> 00:14:32,980
contention.

170
00:14:32,981 --> 00:14:40,060
He was a pragmatist, I should say, who was cognizant all the time of the political aspects

171
00:14:40,061 --> 00:14:44,860
of the Ordnance Corps, the decisions that were made on small arms.

172
00:14:45,340 --> 00:14:50,520
He felt that a worthy end would justify the means in general,

173
00:14:50,521 --> 00:14:54,800
and sometimes he and I did not agree on that philosophically.

174
00:14:55,800 --> 00:14:57,940
They were trying to protect their turf.

175
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:00,860
So they really didn't say much.

176
00:15:00,960 --> 00:15:02,880
They didn't say anything hardly against it,

177
00:15:02,960 --> 00:15:05,100
but there certainly wasn't anything for it.

178
00:15:05,720 --> 00:15:09,320
They just thought, like I say, that this was just a passing fancy,

179
00:15:09,321 --> 00:15:15,060
that the Army would go back and settle down and take the M14.

180
00:15:17,500 --> 00:15:20,520
However, the AR-15 still had its champions.

181
00:15:21,140 --> 00:15:27,540
In 1958, General Wyman ordered the Army to give the AR-15 its first series of official tests.

182
00:15:30,160 --> 00:15:38,500
I first saw it when it was sent to Aberdeen Proving Ground for trials in 1958, I think.

183
00:15:38,501 --> 00:15:41,080
and I was favorably impressed.

184
00:15:41,440 --> 00:15:44,500
We did not find in it any characteristics,

185
00:15:44,960 --> 00:15:46,980
any disadvantages, shortcomings,

186
00:15:47,260 --> 00:15:50,260
that we felt could not be overcome

187
00:15:50,261 --> 00:15:51,860
in the normal course of development.

188
00:15:54,100 --> 00:15:56,300
However, during a round of Army tests

189
00:15:56,301 --> 00:15:58,340
conducted in the Arctic that same year,

190
00:15:58,660 --> 00:16:00,620
the AR-15 failed badly,

191
00:16:01,400 --> 00:16:04,520
chalking up a high rate of inaccuracy and malfunctions.

192
00:16:06,340 --> 00:16:08,180
I think if you look into it,

193
00:16:08,181 --> 00:16:12,200
What you're going to find is that the testing wasn't quite fair.

194
00:16:12,500 --> 00:16:20,580
They were, in fact, rigged to make the M16 or the early AR-15 look bad.

195
00:16:22,360 --> 00:16:25,640
Stoner immediately flew to the test site to investigate.

196
00:16:26,960 --> 00:16:30,120
The sites which are held on with taper pins,

197
00:16:30,121 --> 00:16:32,180
there was no reason for them to ever be disassembled.

198
00:16:32,280 --> 00:16:33,440
They'd been taken off the weapons,

199
00:16:34,360 --> 00:16:37,440
and pieces of welding rod and things were stuck in their place.

200
00:16:38,180 --> 00:16:41,180
which meant that the parts were misaligned,

201
00:16:41,940 --> 00:16:44,380
the gun couldn't function properly,

202
00:16:44,480 --> 00:16:45,940
you certainly couldn't hit anything with them.

203
00:16:47,100 --> 00:16:52,080
According to Stoner, it was another example of the Army's hostility toward the AR-15.

204
00:16:52,880 --> 00:16:57,380
In his 1988 interview, Stoner said that while he was at the Arctic site,

205
00:16:57,780 --> 00:17:00,080
the commanding officer dressed him down.

206
00:17:01,500 --> 00:17:05,400
He says, I think you're doing a great disservice to the United States Army.

207
00:17:05,401 --> 00:17:11,580
and he says by bringing along one of these small caliber weapons,

208
00:17:11,660 --> 00:17:15,220
which is going to be a danger to every American soldier that carries one,

209
00:17:16,220 --> 00:17:19,420
and he says, I can't wait to get your ass off this base

210
00:17:19,421 --> 00:17:22,420
as soon as you can get this thing settled.

211
00:17:22,660 --> 00:17:23,500
Please leave.

212
00:17:23,840 --> 00:17:25,740
The military in general tends to be conservative

213
00:17:25,741 --> 00:17:28,400
because they're dealing with technology

214
00:17:28,401 --> 00:17:33,380
which can kill our own people instead of the enemy if you're not careful.

215
00:17:34,080 --> 00:17:36,900
So, I mean, there is a sound basis for that conservatism,

216
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,840
but often the conservatism goes a little overboard.

217
00:17:40,400 --> 00:17:43,000
And this is what happened with the light-caliber infantry weapon.

218
00:17:45,040 --> 00:17:48,240
Despite praise from Bill Davis and other weapons experts,

219
00:17:48,780 --> 00:17:51,820
Army Ordnance dropped all interest in Stoner's new gun.

220
00:17:53,080 --> 00:17:56,840
In 1958, Frederick Carton, head of ordnance,

221
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:58,360
wrote in his official report

222
00:17:58,361 --> 00:18:02,640
that the AR-15 had not demonstrated sufficient technical merit

223
00:18:02,641 --> 00:18:05,120
and should not be developed by the Army.

224
00:18:06,640 --> 00:18:09,760
I thought it was a very bad decision

225
00:18:09,761 --> 00:18:14,500
because I thought the weapon showed great promise

226
00:18:14,501 --> 00:18:18,180
and it certainly needed further development.

227
00:18:19,720 --> 00:18:23,600
The AR-15 seemed finished as a U.S. military weapon.

228
00:18:24,280 --> 00:18:27,360
The gun that would be the M16 would have to wait two years

229
00:18:27,361 --> 00:18:28,640
to fight its next battle,

230
00:18:28,641 --> 00:18:33,180
This time at a 4th of July picnic at a Pennsylvania farm.

231
00:18:37,120 --> 00:18:42,040
In the mid-1960s, Mattel sold a realistic toy version of the M16 rifle.

232
00:18:42,460 --> 00:18:45,640
This helped fuel a popular belief among American soldiers in Vietnam

233
00:18:45,641 --> 00:18:47,800
that their weapons were made by the toy maker.

234
00:18:48,340 --> 00:18:50,220
The myth continues even to this day.

235
00:18:50,540 --> 00:18:53,140
The M16 will return on Modern Marvels.

236
00:18:53,141 --> 00:19:00,440
Even though the U.S. Army had turned down the AR-15,

237
00:19:01,100 --> 00:19:04,240
Colt Patent Firearms Company bought the rights from Armalite.

238
00:19:04,800 --> 00:19:08,580
They started aggressively marketing the AR-15 around the world,

239
00:19:09,340 --> 00:19:13,140
billing it both as a sporting gun and as a military rifle.

240
00:19:15,760 --> 00:19:20,180
The company got some unexpected help from Air Force General Curtis LeMay,

241
00:19:20,181 --> 00:19:25,060
one of the most controversial, outspoken, and powerful men in Washington.

242
00:19:26,240 --> 00:19:31,320
I should say probably General LeMay was instrumental more than anyone else

243
00:19:31,321 --> 00:19:37,560
in getting the attention of the military to the AR-15.

244
00:19:38,900 --> 00:19:43,720
In the summer of 1960, General LeMay went to a Fourth of July picnic

245
00:19:43,721 --> 00:19:46,340
hosted by representatives of the gun industry.

246
00:19:46,341 --> 00:19:53,800
A Colt salesman set up three watermelons at 50 yards, 100 yards, and 150 yards.

247
00:19:54,260 --> 00:19:57,100
Then he handed the general an AR-15.

248
00:20:04,800 --> 00:20:09,600
General LeMay thought the AR-15 would be perfect for defending Air Force bases.

249
00:20:10,300 --> 00:20:13,080
He ordered 80,000 on the spot.

250
00:20:13,081 --> 00:20:17,400
Congress, however, put LeMay's order on hold.

251
00:20:18,080 --> 00:20:19,360
The country was at peace.

252
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:21,360
Why spend money on war?

253
00:20:26,240 --> 00:20:29,080
Seven months later, in January of 1961,

254
00:20:30,260 --> 00:20:34,440
Dwight D. Eisenhower handed over the office of president to John F. Kennedy.

255
00:20:36,360 --> 00:20:38,500
Let every nation know,

256
00:20:38,501 --> 00:20:43,160
whether it wishes us well or ill,

257
00:20:44,240 --> 00:20:46,900
that we shall pay any price,

258
00:20:48,120 --> 00:20:49,500
bear any burden,

259
00:20:50,780 --> 00:20:52,040
meet any hardship,

260
00:20:53,380 --> 00:20:54,900
support any friend,

261
00:20:55,900 --> 00:20:57,620
oppose any foe

262
00:20:57,621 --> 00:21:01,480
to assure the survival and the success of liberty.

263
00:21:03,120 --> 00:21:06,560
Those words would be put to the test sooner than expected.

264
00:21:07,380 --> 00:21:12,400
Eisenhower also handed Kennedy a growing problem in a faraway country called Vietnam.

265
00:21:15,140 --> 00:21:21,540
At that time, there were about 700 American advisors in Vietnam training South Vietnamese soldiers.

266
00:21:22,600 --> 00:21:27,240
Through roundabout channels, the AR-15s Colt had been selling in the private sector

267
00:21:27,241 --> 00:21:29,840
had found their way into military hands.

268
00:21:29,841 --> 00:21:39,340
There was an army research operation that bought like, oh, 750 or 1,000 of the weapons

269
00:21:39,341 --> 00:21:43,240
and tested them in different ways in different places.

270
00:21:43,820 --> 00:21:47,800
And amongst the places they tested them were in Vietnam,

271
00:21:48,480 --> 00:21:52,960
where we had military assistance people advising the Vietnamese.

272
00:21:52,961 --> 00:21:59,740
And they put some of these weapons into the hands of a ranger battalion in Vietnam

273
00:21:59,741 --> 00:22:03,880
and the Rangers used them under combat conditions.

274
00:22:04,400 --> 00:22:08,860
They thought that this weapon was the best thing they'd ever seen.

275
00:22:08,861 --> 00:22:13,100
So it began to get introduced a bit at a time

276
00:22:13,101 --> 00:22:18,000
and informally outside the regular procurement process.

277
00:22:18,780 --> 00:22:24,300
And the more that it got used by troops, the more troops liked it.

278
00:22:25,120 --> 00:22:29,100
The gun's lightweight and full automatic capability

279
00:22:29,101 --> 00:22:31,320
made it ideal for jungle fighting.

280
00:22:32,760 --> 00:22:37,100
Combat in Vietnam was largely what the military calls meeting engagements.

281
00:22:37,780 --> 00:22:39,360
And indeed, this is very common for the infantry.

282
00:22:39,460 --> 00:22:43,280
Meeting engagement is where you bump into the enemy, not expecting the other guy.

283
00:22:43,680 --> 00:22:46,280
The second most common form of combat is the ambush,

284
00:22:46,800 --> 00:22:48,860
where the other guy bumps into you, but you know he's coming.

285
00:22:49,220 --> 00:22:51,740
In both cases, you have the element of surprise.

286
00:22:51,880 --> 00:22:54,040
In a meeting engagement, both sides have the element of surprise.

287
00:22:54,380 --> 00:22:57,720
So whoever gets out the most firepower first will win.

288
00:22:59,100 --> 00:23:06,460
Word of the AR-15's popularity in Vietnam got back to Secretary of Defense Robert McNamara.

289
00:23:06,920 --> 00:23:14,000
He and his crew of Ivy League-educated, computer-savvy assistants known as the WizKids were immediately intrigued.

290
00:23:15,980 --> 00:23:20,880
McNamara and his people decided they wanted that weapon.

291
00:23:21,580 --> 00:23:25,720
They looked at the history of it, decided that this was fundamentally a good weapon,

292
00:23:25,721 --> 00:23:34,420
that the special uses it had been tested through in Vietnam and elsewhere showed that this was an incredibly good weapon.

293
00:23:34,820 --> 00:23:40,260
They knew that the Army ordnance people were dragging their feet,

294
00:23:40,980 --> 00:23:46,120
and therefore they just said, let's move this weapon.

295
00:23:47,700 --> 00:23:53,780
McNamara was a former executive at Ford Motor Company and a firm believer in the beauties of mass production.

296
00:23:53,781 --> 00:23:58,540
Robert McNamara started asking basic fundamental questions like,

297
00:23:59,140 --> 00:24:03,980
why does every branch of the service wear different shoes?

298
00:24:04,760 --> 00:24:06,940
Why can't you have one pair of shoes and save the money?

299
00:24:07,680 --> 00:24:09,180
Why do you wear different uniforms?

300
00:24:09,380 --> 00:24:10,500
Why do you fly different airplanes?

301
00:24:11,460 --> 00:24:15,860
Well, there's some very good reasons why the services fly different airplanes.

302
00:24:16,460 --> 00:24:19,960
You can't find a very good reason why everybody shouldn't wear the same shoes.

303
00:24:20,460 --> 00:24:22,960
But he also asked the same question about weaponry.

304
00:24:23,780 --> 00:24:26,880
Why can't everybody have the same weapon?

305
00:24:27,660 --> 00:24:30,400
He's Secretary of Defense. He says, you will produce this weapon.

306
00:24:30,680 --> 00:24:32,780
You will distribute this weapon to the troops.

307
00:24:33,280 --> 00:24:36,940
The Ordinance Corps had no choice at that point but to salute and say, yes, sir.

308
00:24:38,160 --> 00:24:42,660
McNamara ordered the Army, Air Force, and Marines to do something unprecedented.

309
00:24:43,160 --> 00:24:45,260
He ordered them to cooperate.

310
00:24:46,260 --> 00:24:50,580
Get the AR-15 combat ready as quickly and efficiently as possible.

311
00:24:51,100 --> 00:24:53,100
One gun for all.

312
00:24:53,780 --> 00:25:06,220
Those of us who had occasion to cover the Pentagon couldn't help but be impressed by the determination of McNamara and the people under them to do what they thought

313
00:25:06,221 --> 00:25:12,440
was right, correctly or otherwise, to cut through the legendary military red tape.

314
00:25:13,560 --> 00:25:16,040
It was a good idea in theory.

315
00:25:16,041 --> 00:25:23,040
The Ordinance and Weapons People thought they knew how to run a weapons program despite

316
00:25:23,041 --> 00:25:31,040
the historic difficulties that Ordinance had in developing really effective small arms

317
00:25:31,041 --> 00:25:32,075
and rifles.

318
00:25:32,076 --> 00:25:39,040
And the defense community that Secretary McNamara personified thought that

319
00:25:39,041 --> 00:25:44,860
they knew what to do and that the Army Ordinance People were obstructions.

320
00:25:44,861 --> 00:25:46,040
Neither side of

321
00:25:46,041 --> 00:25:52,680
would bend over backwards and so accommodation just wasn't a word that got used in those days.

322
00:25:54,220 --> 00:25:59,320
Consensus was very hard to come by. Even when the various armed services agreed,

323
00:25:59,660 --> 00:26:02,704
they were often overruled by McNamara's whiz kids.

324
00:26:02,705 --> 00:26:05,140
Though the whiz kids had little previous

325
00:26:05,141 --> 00:26:11,203
experience with weaponry, they made all the final decisions.

326
00:26:11,204 --> 00:26:14,740
They tended to have a great deal of

327
00:26:14,741 --> 00:26:21,600
confidence in their own intellectual superiority, and they tended to have an attitude which I can

328
00:26:21,601 --> 00:26:27,572
only describe as arrogant. Those guys were really computer-oriented.

329
00:26:27,573 --> 00:26:29,680
They were numbers crazy.

330
00:26:30,500 --> 00:26:35,741
And you can argue that the war in Vietnam was run by the numbers.

331
00:26:35,742 --> 00:26:38,080
For instance, if you expended

332
00:26:38,081 --> 00:26:42,920
X amount of ammunition, you were expected to kill Y amount of folks.

333
00:26:44,960 --> 00:26:47,060
It was stupid. It was ridiculous.

334
00:26:47,360 --> 00:26:49,840
It was dumb.

335
00:26:52,300 --> 00:26:54,780
McNamara's whiz kids argued with the military

336
00:26:54,781 --> 00:26:58,060
over 130 proposed changes to the AR-15,

337
00:26:58,640 --> 00:27:00,900
which would soon be renamed the M-16.

338
00:27:01,740 --> 00:27:04,400
Several of those changes would prove problematic.

339
00:27:04,401 --> 00:27:11,300
This is the M16A1, which is the rifle that was used in Vietnam.

340
00:27:12,420 --> 00:27:17,220
The distinguishing characteristic is this forward assist, which is right here on the rifle.

341
00:27:17,940 --> 00:27:19,660
It had a 20-round magazine.

342
00:27:20,240 --> 00:27:28,180
The troops never put more than 16 in because of the spring here in the magazine would wear out,

343
00:27:28,280 --> 00:27:29,340
so you didn't want to do that.

344
00:27:29,480 --> 00:27:34,040
Notice that it has a left and a right hand grip up here.

345
00:27:34,400 --> 00:27:44,120
And it also has a three-pronged flash suppressor, but if you notice that this barrel is considerably thicker than the one in the original AR-15 rifle.

346
00:27:44,800 --> 00:27:51,800
The other thing about this weapon is that it has a selector switch that right now it's in safe.

347
00:27:52,200 --> 00:27:56,580
Right here is semi-automatic, and right there it's fully automatic.

348
00:27:59,500 --> 00:28:04,300
The new M16 was different from the AR-15 in one other significant way.

349
00:28:04,400 --> 00:28:11,400
The kind of gunpowder used in the M16 cartridges was switched from stick powder to ball powder.

350
00:28:11,401 --> 00:28:17,400
Ball powder gave the M16 a little more of the long-range capacity that the Army so loved.

351
00:28:20,400 --> 00:28:26,400
While the development of the M16 continued, American involvement in Vietnam escalated.

352
00:28:27,400 --> 00:28:33,400
By mid-1962, the number of American advisors had jumped from 700 to 12,000.

353
00:28:34,400 --> 00:28:38,280
Soon, those advisors would leave the sidelines.

354
00:28:38,281 --> 00:28:43,280
Full-out combat was just ahead.

355
00:28:43,281 --> 00:28:51,520
By 1963, the AR-15 had been fighting its way through the Army Ordnance System for five years,

356
00:28:51,521 --> 00:28:59,520
and the need for the gun was becoming increasingly clear.

357
00:28:59,521 --> 00:29:06,520
In May of that year, the crisis in South Vietnam intensified dramatically.

358
00:29:06,521 --> 00:29:12,520
South Vietnamese President Ngo Dinh Diem alienated the Buddhist majority and the world

359
00:29:12,521 --> 00:29:16,520
by attacking temples and shooting anti-Diem demonstrators.

360
00:29:16,521 --> 00:29:20,520
Buddhist priests burned themselves alive in protest.

361
00:29:20,521 --> 00:29:31,080
The North Vietnamese communists gained territory all over the country, and the number of American

362
00:29:31,081 --> 00:29:37,720
advisers reached 15,000.

363
00:29:37,721 --> 00:29:44,080
In late October 1963, mutinous South Vietnamese generals staged a coup against President

364
00:29:44,081 --> 00:29:45,620
Diem.

365
00:29:45,621 --> 00:29:50,094
The next day, he was murdered, just three weeks before John F.

366
00:29:50,095 --> 00:29:52,620
Kennedy was assassinated in Dallas.

367
00:29:57,620 --> 00:30:05,620
August 2nd, 1964, North Vietnamese gunboats engaged an American destroyer, the Maddox, in the Tonkin Gulf.

368
00:30:05,621 --> 00:30:19,621
In March of 1965, the first official US combat troops were deployed in Vietnam.

369
00:30:21,420 --> 00:30:26,840
More soldiers would be on their way, and all of them needed guns.

370
00:30:26,841 --> 00:30:33,160
The M16 was finally going to war.

371
00:30:33,161 --> 00:30:39,300
In January 1966, there were 200,000 American troops in Vietnam.

372
00:30:39,301 --> 00:30:45,340
By year's end, the number had doubled.

373
00:30:45,341 --> 00:30:48,840
The M16s were now in full production.

374
00:30:48,841 --> 00:30:55,000
At the peak, 45,000 guns came off the Colt assembly lines every month.

375
00:30:55,001 --> 00:31:01,540
During the summer of 1966, the M16 was issued to the troops at large for the first time.

376
00:31:04,860 --> 00:31:09,380
Only a few months later, soldiers started reporting malfunctions.

377
00:31:10,900 --> 00:31:17,380
They were jamming in combat, in the jungle, in defensive postures.

378
00:31:18,060 --> 00:31:20,400
The weapon would jam.

379
00:31:20,401 --> 00:31:27,980
When you had these stoppages, the only way you could get the thing to work again

380
00:31:27,981 --> 00:31:34,500
was to quite literally tear it down and clean it right there on the battlefield.

381
00:31:35,080 --> 00:31:40,100
And in the middle of a firefight, it's not a good idea to tear your weapon apart,

382
00:31:40,820 --> 00:31:45,200
particularly one that has all the little parts of the M16 rifle at night.

383
00:31:45,201 --> 00:31:55,220
so naturally you're going from an m14 which would work to one that you have these problems with

384
00:31:55,221 --> 00:31:57,640
and of course the troops hated the thing

385
00:31:57,641 --> 00:32:06,600
many of them had gotten the weapon at the last minute hadn't been sufficiently trained on it

386
00:32:06,601 --> 00:32:12,640
had been told to go out on their perimeters and fire some blasts off into the jungle

387
00:32:12,641 --> 00:32:16,760
and that was the most exposure they'd gotten to the weapon.

388
00:32:17,480 --> 00:32:18,540
They didn't have training.

389
00:32:20,300 --> 00:32:23,640
Though every gun in history has required careful maintenance,

390
00:32:24,240 --> 00:32:26,680
troops were told the M16 was the exception.

391
00:32:27,380 --> 00:32:28,440
It was self-cleaning.

392
00:32:29,240 --> 00:32:30,880
Cleaning kits weren't even issued.

393
00:32:31,600 --> 00:32:34,340
The result? Dirty, gummed-up guns.

394
00:32:36,020 --> 00:32:39,080
Supply stations in Vietnam started getting calls

395
00:32:39,081 --> 00:32:42,500
for an emergency resupply of both ammunition and rifles.

396
00:32:42,640 --> 00:32:47,800
Ted Reno, then a maintenance officer, remembers one night in particular.

397
00:32:49,360 --> 00:32:57,520
They said, we need X amount of ammunition and 21 M16s, which kind of red light goes on.

398
00:32:57,600 --> 00:33:00,480
You've got 26 non-operational weapons that they want to replace.

399
00:33:01,360 --> 00:33:05,100
And of course, later on, the message was, you need to check them out because there's something,

400
00:33:05,280 --> 00:33:07,780
there's definitely a defect in either the ammunition or the weapon.

401
00:33:08,780 --> 00:33:13,860
We don't know how many troops were killed as a direct result of the fouling and the jamming problems with the M-16.

402
00:33:14,160 --> 00:33:15,049
We'll probably never know.

403
00:33:15,050 --> 00:33:17,680
It's not the sort of thing the Army keeps track of for any number of reasons.

404
00:33:18,120 --> 00:33:19,400
But it did happen.

405
00:33:21,760 --> 00:33:23,660
The troops were outraged.

406
00:33:24,300 --> 00:33:30,920
One soldier bypassed the chain of command and sent Senator Gaylord Nelson of Wisconsin a letter detailing the horror.

407
00:33:32,340 --> 00:33:36,460
The weapon has failed us at crucial moments when we needed firepower most.

408
00:33:37,340 --> 00:33:40,840
In each case, it left Marines naked against their enemy.

409
00:33:41,520 --> 00:33:46,180
In each case, and this is no exaggeration, we take counts after each fight.

410
00:33:46,780 --> 00:33:49,700
As many as 50% of the rifles fail to work.

411
00:33:50,320 --> 00:33:55,400
I know of at least two Marines who died within 10 feet of the enemy with jammed rifles.

412
00:33:58,580 --> 00:34:00,700
The infantry troop didn't have to see it.

413
00:34:01,080 --> 00:34:02,360
All they had to do was hear about it.

414
00:34:02,740 --> 00:34:03,820
And it hurt morale.

415
00:34:04,140 --> 00:34:06,380
It probably hurt morale more than it hurt anything else.

416
00:34:06,460 --> 00:34:10,420
because basically the one thing an infantry soldier cannot lose faith in is his weapon.

417
00:34:12,220 --> 00:34:16,260
Congressman Richard Eichord of Missouri launched an in-depth investigation.

418
00:34:17,220 --> 00:34:23,120
His final report blamed the problem on the Army's decision to switch from stick powder to ball powder.

419
00:34:24,940 --> 00:34:28,720
Ball powder made the M-16 fire at a higher rate of speed.

420
00:34:29,240 --> 00:34:31,540
That produced more wear and tear on the weapon.

421
00:34:32,400 --> 00:34:34,080
More breakdowns were inevitable,

422
00:34:34,780 --> 00:34:38,460
especially since ball powder burned dirtier, fouling the barrel.

423
00:34:43,760 --> 00:34:48,300
And not only did it foul, but it was susceptible to moisture.

424
00:34:49,260 --> 00:34:53,640
So one of the standing operating procedures, at least in my unit in Vietnam,

425
00:34:54,060 --> 00:34:56,620
was that you would never put a round in the chamber overnight

426
00:34:56,621 --> 00:35:00,400
because the round would swell and you couldn't get it out.

427
00:35:00,401 --> 00:35:05,600
Quite literally, the back end of the cartridge would be ripped out,

428
00:35:06,000 --> 00:35:10,100
and then if you got another one up in there, then you were in deep, deep, deep, deep trouble.

429
00:35:12,680 --> 00:35:15,120
Though ball powder was the official culprit,

430
00:35:15,560 --> 00:35:19,080
it may not have been the real cause of the M-16's problems.

431
00:35:20,840 --> 00:35:23,960
The principal problem was a failure to chrome plate the chamber.

432
00:35:23,961 --> 00:35:32,420
That was the cause of the serious malfunctions that caused the controversy in Vietnam.

433
00:35:33,700 --> 00:35:37,660
The Army had known since it fought in the Pacific during World War II

434
00:35:37,661 --> 00:35:43,480
that chrome plating the chamber eliminates rust, corrosion, and pitting that can make a gun jam.

435
00:35:44,280 --> 00:35:47,660
However, McNamara's whiz kids vetoed the idea.

436
00:35:47,661 --> 00:35:55,620
their attitude was that if the gun had needed a chrome-plated chamber stoner would have designed

437
00:35:55,621 --> 00:36:01,020
it that way in the first place and it didn't have one therefore it didn't need one the army was just

438
00:36:01,021 --> 00:36:06,680
trying to delay the implementation of its recommendation to get on with production

439
00:36:06,681 --> 00:36:12,940
few at the i-court hearings understood the technicalities of weapon design and manufacture

440
00:36:12,941 --> 00:36:17,940
When the finger-pointing started, few knew exactly where to lay the blame.

441
00:36:17,941 --> 00:36:20,940
Some said the fault lay with McNamara and his aides.

442
00:36:20,941 --> 00:36:24,940
For others, the buck stopped at the Army's front door.

443
00:36:26,940 --> 00:36:30,940
McNamara was responsible for a lot of sins, but the M16 is not one of them.

444
00:36:30,941 --> 00:36:35,940
He basically turned to the Ordnance Corps, whose job is to produce the weapons in working order,

445
00:36:35,941 --> 00:36:37,940
and said, see this AR-15?

446
00:36:37,941 --> 00:36:40,940
Get a military version out in the field quickly

447
00:36:40,941 --> 00:36:42,940
for all the infantry troops.

448
00:36:42,941 --> 00:36:44,940
I mean, that's a fairly clear order.

449
00:36:44,941 --> 00:36:47,940
And the Ordnance Corps had the means to execute that order.

450
00:36:47,941 --> 00:36:49,940
They did not.

451
00:36:50,940 --> 00:36:53,940
The Army started issuing cleaning kits immediately

452
00:36:53,941 --> 00:36:58,940
and quietly began retrofitting M16s with chrome-plated bores.

453
00:36:58,941 --> 00:37:02,940
Within a year, the malfunctions were largely a thing of the past.

454
00:37:02,941 --> 00:37:08,452
past. Troops who came after 1967 encountered little trouble.

455
00:37:08,453 --> 00:37:10,840
I never really had a major

456
00:37:10,841 --> 00:37:11,730
problem with it.

457
00:37:11,731 --> 00:37:15,680
Every once in a while it would jam, but if you kept it clean, you know,

458
00:37:15,681 --> 00:37:21,040
you just disassemble it and clean it, and if you kept it clean, it worked all right.

459
00:37:23,320 --> 00:37:26,144
The problems we ran out with this I don't think were unique.

460
00:37:26,145 --> 00:37:27,320
Probably running with any

461
00:37:27,321 --> 00:37:31,087
new piece of equipment. I can't think of anything that's ever been fielded.

462
00:37:31,088 --> 00:37:31,840
It was perfect.

463
00:37:32,940 --> 00:37:42,260
Once the initial problems were solved with the powder and having a bolt closure on the thing and that, then it became accepted, I think.

464
00:37:42,620 --> 00:37:46,420
The troops liked it, and they would definitely pull the trigger.

465
00:37:48,360 --> 00:37:53,620
Though its beginnings were rocky, the M-16 has gone on to win praise.

466
00:37:54,780 --> 00:37:59,800
The M-16 today is the respected backbone of the U.S. military.

467
00:38:02,940 --> 00:38:09,180
Many innovative weapons, including the M16, were introduced to combat during the Vietnam War.

468
00:38:09,181 --> 00:38:15,340
Among these weapons were the F-4s, the Claymore Mines, the AH-1G Huey Cobra, and the gunship.

469
00:38:15,800 --> 00:38:18,400
The M16 will return on Modern Marvels.

470
00:38:22,720 --> 00:38:31,800
By the time the last American troops left Vietnam, the M16 had become one of the most heavily tested and most reliable weapons in the world.

471
00:38:31,801 --> 00:38:36,881
It has remained a staple of the U.S.

472
00:38:36,882 --> 00:38:44,220
military during action in Grenada, Haiti and Panama.

473
00:38:44,221 --> 00:38:53,340
It was also highly visible during United Nations military operations in Bosnia and Somalia.

474
00:38:53,341 --> 00:38:58,800
During the Gulf War, the M16 took a back seat to the flashy new stealth fighters and Patriot

475
00:38:58,801 --> 00:38:59,800
missiles.

476
00:38:59,801 --> 00:39:05,760
But the maintenance lessons troops learned in Vietnam again paid off.

477
00:39:06,760 --> 00:39:07,460
U.S.

478
00:39:07,461 --> 00:39:14,880
troops used a few old soldiers' tricks to help the M-16 survive blowing grit and sand that would have jammed most other guns.

479
00:39:15,680 --> 00:39:20,500
They used women's nylons and condoms to cover the barrels and keep them free from dirt.

480
00:39:23,360 --> 00:39:26,660
Other improvements have come through more traditional channels.

481
00:39:27,380 --> 00:39:30,440
The current M16 is no longer fully automatic.

482
00:39:33,460 --> 00:39:37,920
Instead, it fires a three-round burst every time the trigger is pulled.

483
00:39:39,620 --> 00:39:43,520
I like the M16 today, the AA2 model.

484
00:39:45,300 --> 00:39:46,720
It's got a very heavy barrel.

485
00:39:46,860 --> 00:39:49,240
It fires different ammunition from the ones we had in Vietnam.

486
00:39:49,920 --> 00:39:51,480
It has a 30-round magazine.

487
00:39:51,481 --> 00:39:57,400
and most of all, I like the fact that you can change elevation and windage

488
00:39:57,401 --> 00:40:01,660
on the back end of the weapon rather than on the front end.

489
00:40:01,820 --> 00:40:03,580
It makes it very, very accurate.

490
00:40:04,660 --> 00:40:07,260
The gun itself is now somewhat heavier

491
00:40:07,261 --> 00:40:10,680
and uses more powerful ammunition to increase its range.

492
00:40:11,420 --> 00:40:16,340
It can also be equipped with a high-tech laser sight, ideal for night battles.

493
00:40:21,480 --> 00:40:29,580
In the future, the M16 assault rifle may be the heart of an even more high-tech fighter, the Land Warrior.

494
00:40:34,380 --> 00:40:39,160
The Land Warrior could carry a shortened M16 with an accessory rail.

495
00:40:40,640 --> 00:40:46,260
Depending on need, a soldier can pop on a thermal scope to spot enemies through their own body heat,

496
00:40:47,100 --> 00:40:49,480
get a beat on them with a night vision scope,

497
00:40:49,481 --> 00:40:53,420
and target them precisely with a laser rangefinder.

498
00:40:55,160 --> 00:40:59,200
A video camera can silently capture every moment of action.

499
00:41:00,060 --> 00:41:04,900
Even fully loaded, land warrior gear is light enough to let troops maneuver comfortably.

500
00:41:06,000 --> 00:41:11,880
The land warrior system will make an unprecedented amount of vital information instantaneously available,

501
00:41:11,881 --> 00:41:17,660
not only to soldiers in the field, but to their commanders, perhaps many miles away.

502
00:41:19,480 --> 00:41:24,520
The M16 is also starting to prove itself indispensable on the urban battlefield.

503
00:41:25,560 --> 00:41:32,100
Until the winter of 1997, Los Angeles police carried only 9mm semi-automatic pistols,

504
00:41:32,720 --> 00:41:35,960
.38 caliber revolvers, and 12-gauge shotguns.

505
00:41:36,440 --> 00:41:37,980
Quite a lot of firepower.

506
00:41:38,340 --> 00:41:44,760
But on February 28, 1997, those weapons left the LAPD feeling naked.

507
00:41:44,761 --> 00:41:53,440
On that day, the police stood their ground in a shootout with two armed robbers making their getaway from a North Hollywood bank.

508
00:41:54,120 --> 00:42:01,260
The robbers wore head-to-toe body armor and carried five fully automatic weapons, including two AK-47s.

509
00:42:02,560 --> 00:42:09,020
During the firefight, police raced to a nearby gun store for anything that could give them a fighting chance.

510
00:42:09,021 --> 00:42:15,320
In the end, their bravery, resourcefulness and fortitude won the day.

511
00:42:16,140 --> 00:42:17,300
The robbers were killed.

512
00:42:18,320 --> 00:42:21,020
No one, however, wanted to see that day repeated.

513
00:42:22,280 --> 00:42:27,040
In September of 1997, California Governor Pete Wilson announced

514
00:42:27,041 --> 00:42:31,140
the state had bought 600 military surplus M-16 assault rifles

515
00:42:31,141 --> 00:42:33,180
for the Los Angeles Police Department.

516
00:42:33,181 --> 00:42:39,180
The M16s may prevent the LAPD from ever again being outgunned.

517
00:42:39,181 --> 00:42:43,180
I think it's an excellent weapon, good reliability,

518
00:42:43,181 --> 00:42:46,180
altogether the best rifle system we've ever had.

519
00:42:46,181 --> 00:42:49,180
There may be something out there that I don't know about,

520
00:42:49,181 --> 00:42:54,180
but so far as I know there's nothing on the horizon that would justify a change.

521
00:42:54,181 --> 00:43:00,180
The bottom line is everybody's tried it, everybody, with that exception, liked it.

522
00:43:00,181 --> 00:43:02,139
exception liked it. Everybody uses it.

523
00:43:02,140 --> 00:43:04,820
The only people who use AK-47s, which are basically,

524
00:43:04,821 --> 00:43:08,740
you know, surplus on the market, are revolutionaries who can't afford an M16.

525
00:43:11,460 --> 00:43:18,660
It's probably extraordinary that a weapon that was a subject of so much controversy when it

526
00:43:18,661 --> 00:43:27,220
got introduced and which so many people thought would be phased out or replaced because of those

527
00:43:27,221 --> 00:43:33,220
kinds of controversies is still around and is still the Army's weapon of choice.

528
00:43:33,221 --> 00:43:39,306
And that's a tribute to something. Maybe it's a tribute to Gene Stoner.

529
00:43:39,307 --> 00:43:42,220
Maybe it's a tribute to the Army's

530
00:43:42,221 --> 00:43:48,415
flexibility in coming to understand this was indeed the weapon of the future.

531
00:43:48,416 --> 00:43:49,220
Who knows?

532
00:43:49,221 --> 00:43:50,220
Who knows?

533
00:43:50,221 --> 00:43:57,700
The M16's glory days may now be limited, however.

534
00:43:57,701 --> 00:44:03,940
In April 1998, the Marine Corps announced it is spending $8.5 million to develop the

535
00:44:03,941 --> 00:44:07,000
objective individual combat weapon.

536
00:44:07,001 --> 00:44:14,280
It will be able to fire two kinds of ammunition, NATO standard 5.56 millimeter and 20 millimeter

537
00:44:14,281 --> 00:44:18,860
high-explosive air-bursting ammunition.

538
00:44:18,861 --> 00:44:22,160
Will it truly be the ultimate infantry weapon?

539
00:44:22,161 --> 00:44:23,580
Perhaps.

540
00:44:23,581 --> 00:44:29,460
But don't be surprised if years from now, the M16 is still the military's gun of choice.

